{"version":"6.9","hitCount":10,"nextCursorMark":"AoIIPgKzHicyOTYzOTE5","nextPageUrl":"https://www.ebi.ac.uk/europepmc/webservices/rest/search?query=TITLE%3A%22nosology+and+pathogenesis%22&cursorMark=AoIIPgKzHicyOTYzOTE5&resultType=core&pageSize=5&format=json","request":{"queryString":"TITLE:\"nosology and pathogenesis\"","resultType":"core","cursorMark":"*","pageSize":5,"sort":"","synonym":false},"resultList":{"result":[{"id":"18932124","source":"MED","pmid":"18932124","doi":"10.1002/ajmg.c.30190","title":"On the nosology and pathogenesis of Wolf-Hirschhorn syndrome: genotype-phenotype correlation analysis of 80 patients and literature review.","authorString":"Zollino M, Murdolo M, Marangi G, Pecile V, Galasso C, Mazzanti L, Neri G.","authorList":{"author":[{"fullName":"Zollino M","lastName":"Zollino","firstName":"Marcella","initials":"M","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0003-4871-9519"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Medical Genetics, Università Cattolica Sacro Cuore, Roma, Italy. mzollino@rm.unicatt.it"}]}},{"fullName":"Murdolo M","lastName":"Murdolo","firstName":"Marina","initials":"M"},{"fullName":"Marangi G","lastName":"Marangi","firstName":"Giuseppe","initials":"G","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-6898-8882"}},{"fullName":"Pecile V","lastName":"Pecile","firstName":"Vanna","initials":"V","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-9045-3761"}},{"fullName":"Galasso C","lastName":"Galasso","firstName":"Cinzia","initials":"C"},{"fullName":"Mazzanti L","lastName":"Mazzanti","firstName":"Laura","initials":"L","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-7670-6494"}},{"fullName":"Neri G","lastName":"Neri","firstName":"Giovanni","initials":"G"}]},"authorIdList":{"authorId":[{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-6898-8882"},{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-7670-6494"},{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-9045-3761"},{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0003-4871-9519"}]},"dataLinksTagsList":{"dataLinkstag":["related_data"]},"journalInfo":{"issue":"4","volume":"148C","journalIssueId":1575899,"dateOfPublication":"2008 Nov","monthOfPublication":11,"yearOfPublication":2008,"journal":{"medlineAbbreviation":"Am J Med Genet C Semin Med Genet","title":"American journal of medical genetics. Part C, Seminars in medical genetics","essn":"1552-4876","issn":"1552-4868","isoabbreviation":"Am J Med Genet C Semin Med Genet","nlmid":"101235745"},"printPublicationDate":"2008-11-01"},"pubYear":"2008","pageInfo":"257-269","abstractText":"Based on genotype-phenotype correlation analysis of 80 Wolf-Hirschhorn syndrome (WHS) patients, as well as on review of relevant literature, we add further insights to the following aspects of WHS: (1) clinical delineation and phenotypic categories; (2) characterization of the basic genomic defect, mechanisms of origin and familiarity; (3) identification of prognostic factors for mental retardation; (4) chromosome mapping of the distinctive clinical signs, in an effort to identify pathogenic genes. Clinically, we consider that minimal diagnostic criteria for WHS, defining a \"core\" phenotype, are typical facial appearance, mental retardation, growth delay and seizures (or EEG anomalies). Three different categories of the WHS phenotype were defined, generally correlating with the extent of the 4p deletion. The first one comprises a small deletion not exceeding 3.5 Mb, that is usually associated with a mild phenotype, lacking major malformations. This category is likely under-diagnosed. The second and by far the more frequent category is identified by large deletions, averaging between 5 and 18 Mb, and causes the widely recognizable WHS phenotype. The third clinical category results from a very large deletion exceeding 22-25 Mb causing a severe phenotype, that can hardly be defined as typical WHS. Genetically, de novo chromosome abnormalities in WHS include pure deletions but also complex rearrangements, mainly unbalanced translocations. With the exception of t(4p;8p), WHS-associated chromosome abnormalities are neither mediated by segmental duplications, nor associated with a parental inversion polymorphism on 4p16.3. Factors involved in prediction of prognosis include the extent of the deletion, the occurrence of complex chromosome anomalies, and the severity of seizures. We found that the core phenotype maps within the terminal 1.9 Mb region of chromosome 4p. Therefore, WHSCR-2 should be considered the critical region for this condition. We also confirmed that the pathogenesis of WHS is multigenic. Specific and independent chromosome regions were characterized for growth delay and seizures, as well as for the additional clinical signs that characterize this condition. With the exception of parental balanced translocations, familial recurrence is uncommon.","affiliation":"Department of Medical Genetics, Università Cattolica Sacro Cuore, Roma, Italy. mzollino@rm.unicatt.it","publicationStatus":"ppublish","language":"eng","pubModel":"Print","pubTypeList":{"pubType":["Research Support, Non-U.S. Gov't","Review","Journal Article"]},"meshHeadingList":{"meshHeading":[{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Chromosomes, Human, Pair 4","meshQualifierList":{"meshQualifier":[{"majorTopic_YN":"N","abbreviation":"GE","qualifierName":"genetics"}]}},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Humans"},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Craniofacial Abnormalities","meshQualifierList":{"meshQualifier":[{"majorTopic_YN":"N","abbreviation":"GE","qualifierName":"genetics"},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","abbreviation":"PA","qualifierName":"pathology"}]}},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Chromosome Deletion"},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Translocation, Genetic"},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Prognosis"},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Chromosome Mapping"},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Gene Rearrangement"},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Genotype"},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Phenotype"},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Child"},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Female"},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Male"},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Wolf-Hirschhorn Syndrome","meshQualifierList":{"meshQualifier":[{"majorTopic_YN":"Y","abbreviation":"ET","qualifierName":"etiology"},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","abbreviation":"GE","qualifierName":"genetics"},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","abbreviation":"PA","qualifierName":"pathology"}]}},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Intellectual Disability","meshQualifierList":{"meshQualifier":[{"majorTopic_YN":"N","abbreviation":"GE","qualifierName":"genetics"}]}}]},"subsetList":{"subset":[{"code":"IM","name":"Index Medicus"}]},"fullTextUrlList":{"fullTextUrl":[{"availability":"Subscription required","availabilityCode":"S","documentStyle":"doi","site":"DOI","url":"https://doi.org/10.1002/ajmg.c.30190"}]},"isOpenAccess":"N","inEPMC":"N","inPMC":"N","citedByCount":109,"hasReferences":"Y","hasTextMinedTerms":"Y","hasDbCrossReferences":"N","hasPDF":"N","hasBook":"N","hasSuppl":"N","hasLabsLinks":"Y","hasData":"Y","authMan":"N","epmcAuthMan":"N","nihAuthMan":"N","hasTMAccessionNumbers":"N","dateOfCompletion":"2008-12-09","dateOfCreation":"2008-10-22","dateOfRevision":"2011-11-17","firstPublicationDate":"2008-11-01","firstIndexDate":"2008-12-24","hasEvaluations":"N"},{"id":"8264171","source":"MED","pmid":"8264171","title":"[Congenital stenosis of the anus and rectum (Problems of nosology and pathogenesis)].","authorString":"Pankevich TL, Lëniushkin AI, Alekseevskikh IuG.","authorList":{"author":[{"fullName":"Pankevich TL","lastName":"Pankevich","firstName":"T L","initials":"TL"},{"fullName":"Lëniushkin AI","lastName":"Lëniushkin","firstName":"A I","initials":"AI"},{"fullName":"Alekseevskikh IuG","lastName":"Alekseevskikh","firstName":"Iu G","initials":"IuG"}]},"journalInfo":{"issue":"8","journalIssueId":727517,"dateOfPublication":"1993 Aug","monthOfPublication":8,"yearOfPublication":1993,"journal":{"medlineAbbreviation":"Khirurgiia (Mosk)","title":"Khirurgiia","essn":"2309-5628","issn":"0023-1207","isoabbreviation":"Khirurgiia (Mosk)","nlmid":"0412765"},"printPublicationDate":"1993-08-01"},"pubYear":"1993","pageInfo":"53-59","abstractText":"The work deals with the differential analysis of two forms of congenital rectal stenosis--\"membranous\" which is consequent upon incomplete obliteration of the anal membrane and \"tubular\" with involvement of the rectum along its length. It is pointed out that in most cases tubular rectal stenosis is a component of the complex of anomalies of the caudal region described for the first time by G. Currarino (1981) and called the ASP-triad. From analysis of 12 cases of the ASP syndrome a new scheme of the pathogenesis of the complex of anomalies constituting the syndrome is suggested. The authors identified a component of the syndrome which was not described earlier, namely, specific dysplasia of the pelvic floor and the external sphincter muscles of the anus, which provided the basis for naming the complex the PFASP syndrome (PF--Pelvic Floor Dysplasia). Problems of surgical correction of the PFASP complex of anomalies are discussed. A pathogenetically substantiated analysis of postoperative complications is made.","publicationStatus":"ppublish","language":"rus","pubModel":"Print","pubTypeList":{"pubType":["Journal Article","English Abstract","Case Reports"]},"meshHeadingList":{"meshHeading":[{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Pelvic Floor","meshQualifierList":{"meshQualifier":[{"majorTopic_YN":"Y","abbreviation":"AB","qualifierName":"abnormalities"},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","abbreviation":"SU","qualifierName":"surgery"}]}},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Humans"},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Rectal Diseases","meshQualifierList":{"meshQualifier":[{"majorTopic_YN":"N","abbreviation":"CL","qualifierName":"classification"},{"majorTopic_YN":"Y","abbreviation":"CN","qualifierName":"congenital"},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","abbreviation":"DI","qualifierName":"diagnosis"},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","abbreviation":"SU","qualifierName":"surgery"}]}},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Anus Diseases","meshQualifierList":{"meshQualifier":[{"majorTopic_YN":"N","abbreviation":"CL","qualifierName":"classification"},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","abbreviation":"CN","qualifierName":"congenital"},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","abbreviation":"DI","qualifierName":"diagnosis"},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","abbreviation":"SU","qualifierName":"surgery"}]}},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Constriction, Pathologic"},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Syndrome"},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Child"},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Child, Preschool"},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Infant"},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Female"}]},"subsetList":{"subset":[{"code":"IM","name":"Index Medicus"}]},"isOpenAccess":"N","inEPMC":"N","inPMC":"N","citedByCount":1,"hasReferences":"N","hasTextMinedTerms":"Y","hasDbCrossReferences":"N","hasPDF":"N","hasBook":"N","hasSuppl":"N","hasLabsLinks":"N","hasData":"N","authMan":"N","epmcAuthMan":"N","nihAuthMan":"N","hasTMAccessionNumbers":"N","dateOfCompletion":"1994-01-24","dateOfCreation":"1993-08-01","dateOfRevision":"2006-11-15","firstPublicationDate":"1993-08-01","firstIndexDate":"2008-01-29","hasEvaluations":"N"},{"id":"6333680","source":"MED","pmid":"6333680","doi":"10.3109/15513818309048288","title":"Spontaneous regression of congenital cutaneous histiocytosis X: report of a case with discussion of nosology and pathogenesis.","authorString":"Dehner LP, Bamford JT, McDonald EC.","authorList":{"author":[{"fullName":"Dehner LP","lastName":"Dehner","firstName":"L P","initials":"LP"},{"fullName":"Bamford JT","lastName":"Bamford","firstName":"J T","initials":"JT"},{"fullName":"McDonald EC","lastName":"McDonald","firstName":"E C","initials":"EC"}]},"journalInfo":{"issue":"1","volume":"1","journalIssueId":596903,"dateOfPublication":"1983 Jan-Mar","monthOfPublication":0,"yearOfPublication":1983,"journal":{"medlineAbbreviation":"Pediatr Pathol","title":"Pediatric pathology","issn":"0277-0938","isoabbreviation":"Pediatr Pathol","nlmid":"8303527"},"printPublicationDate":"1983-01-01"},"pubYear":"1983","pageInfo":"99-106","abstractText":"Spontaneous regression of histiocytosis X is a rare biologic event as judged by the extensive recorded experience in the literature. We present a case of congenital cutaneous histiocytosis X that resolved without specific therapy in the first week of life. Histologic and ultrastructural studies confirmed the interpretation. This child is an example of so-called pure cutaneous histiocytosis, which, like the other unisystem forms of the disorders, has an excellent prognosis.","publicationStatus":"ppublish","language":"eng","pubModel":"Print","pubTypeList":{"pubType":["Journal Article","Case Reports"]},"meshHeadingList":{"meshHeading":[{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Humans"},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Histiocytosis, Langerhans-Cell","meshQualifierList":{"meshQualifier":[{"majorTopic_YN":"Y","abbreviation":"PA","qualifierName":"pathology"}]}},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Skin Diseases","meshQualifierList":{"meshQualifier":[{"majorTopic_YN":"Y","abbreviation":"PA","qualifierName":"pathology"}]}},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Microscopy, Electron"},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Prognosis"},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Remission, Spontaneous"},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Infant, Newborn"},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Female"}]},"subsetList":{"subset":[{"code":"IM","name":"Index Medicus"}]},"fullTextUrlList":{"fullTextUrl":[{"availability":"Subscription required","availabilityCode":"S","documentStyle":"doi","site":"DOI","url":"https://doi.org/10.3109/15513818309048288"}]},"isOpenAccess":"N","inEPMC":"N","inPMC":"N","citedByCount":20,"hasReferences":"Y","hasTextMinedTerms":"Y","hasDbCrossReferences":"N","hasPDF":"N","hasBook":"N","hasSup